<sub>Screenshot from a demo DSH instance with sample data.</sub>

A [DeepSeek Harness](https://www.deepseek.com) (DSH) web-profile plugin that tells you which of your installed plugins have a newer release **that your running DSH can actually take**. It only notifies. It never installs, never edits your profile, never runs a child process.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-plugin-updates README · Install (DSH web profile)：Install from npm: `dsh plugin --profile web add @lucagiftzek/dsh-plugin-updates`, or from GitHub: `dsh plugin --profile web add github:lucagiftzek/dsh-plugin-updates`.

```bash
dsh plugin --profile web add @lucagiftzek/dsh-plugin-updates

@lucagiftzek/dsh-plugin-updates README · How it decides compatibility：1. **Classify by specifier.** `registry` (semver range, tag, `npm:` alias) is checked on npm. `github:` and `git+https://github.com/...` are checked against GitHub tags and the default branch HEAD, with the pinned commit read from `pnpm-lock.yaml`. A `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/releases/download/<tag>/<file>.tgz` URL is checked against GitHub releases. `link:`, `file:`, `workspace:` are local: they are compared with an upstream only when the `upstream` map names them, or with their own github.com origin when `checkOwnRemotes` is on. Anything else (another git host, another tarball host) is shown as `manual`.
2. **Gate against the running core.** For each candidate version: if its `engines.dsh` is declared, that range decides alone; otherwise every `@deepseek-ai/dsh` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-*` peer range must match. Matching uses `semver.satisfies(running, range, { includePrerelease: true })`, so `>=0.2.1` refuses an rc and `>=0.2.1-0` accepts it. The DSH 0.1.5 core has no gate of its own, so there the result is "declared compatible", not "verified". Nested `dsh.engines.dsh` ranges are shown as a hint only.
